 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfDPSkRSZrp/",
 "title": "The Art of Getting Recognized for Your Work",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator explains that doing great work is not enough because managers and leadership lack full visibility due to bandwidth constraints. He shares three practical strategies to make sharing achievements easier, including framing them as updates and keeping a record.",
 "key_points": [
  "Nobody has full visibility into your work; managers see a fraction and leadership sees summaries of summaries.",
  "People often say 'just do great work and let it speak for itself' to avoid the discomfort of talking about their own accomplishments.",
  "Share updates instead of bragging by explaining what was done, why it was done, and what the effect was.",
  "Keep a record of your accomplishments so you do not forget them, which is helpful for performance reviews.",
  "Be generous with credit by celebrating other people, as people will still associate you with the win."
 ],
 "content": "Instagram reel/post by Alec Fullmer | Career Success Made Simple. Text / caption: The key to getting the credit you deserve. Make a note of this post. Nobody has full visibility into your work. Your manager sees a fraction of what you do, filtered through whatever meetings you're in together and whatever you mentioned to them. Leadership sees almost nothing directly, they see summaries of summaries written by other people. And that gets filtered through whatever narratives and opinions people have about you. And since I've been making content on the internet, this fact, this reality that doing your job and being recognized for your job are two different efforts is probably the thing that people resist the most. We all wish that the system would just see merit, but it rarely does. And it's not out of malice, it's not because people don't like you, or they don't want to know what you're up to. It's just a bandwidth thing. People don't have time to do everything they need to do and keep up on everything you've done. I can't count the number of times I've heard, I'll just do great work and let it speak for itself. People love believing that that's going to work, because it gives them an excuse to avoid something that's uncomfortable, which is talking about your own accomplishments. I'm not trying to say I'm any different. It's always been difficult for me to proactively share my impact. But here's three things that have made it easier for me. Number one, not thinking about it as sharing my accomplishments, but as just sharing updates. I'm just keeping people up to date on what's been done, why we did it, and what the effect was. Number two, keeping a record of my accomplishments because I'll forget them if I don't. And it's been great to have an archive to go back to specifically for the times that are for bragging about your work, performance reviews and such. And the last thing is being generous with credit. It's always easier to share accomplishments if there are a way to celebrate other people. And it's still two birds with one stone. Like, people will still associate you with the win, even if you give credit to other people.",
 "status": "done",
